//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="hello, wolf")
//! phase: mem
//! conforms: str.interp
//!
//! The most user-visible refusal in the language (reported mid-c08): a
//! function RETURNING an interpolated string. The checked lane runs it
//! today — `eval_string` materializes the built `str` as a value — and
//! the run expectation is proven by its checked-lane twin
//! (`crates/wolf_mem/tests/str_builtins.rs::interpolated_str_as_a_value`).
//! The native lane refuses honestly ("interpolated strings in value
//! position — allocating materialization"): building the string at run
//! time needs the region-backed strbuf design (I15's `#[noalloc]` law
//! makes the allocation story a decision, not a shortcut), which is
//! why this file pins `mem` while carrying its run expectation.

fn greet(name: str) -> str {
    "hello, {name}"
}

fn main() -> !int {
    print(greet("wolf"))
    0
}
